SAGA

The Saga Pattern is a design pattern used in microservices architecture to manage distributed transactions across multiple services without relying on a traditional two-phase commit (2PC).

Instead of one global transaction, a saga breaks the business process into a series of local transactions. If one step fails, the saga executes compensating transactions to undo the previous successful steps.

Example: E-commerce Order

Suppose a customer places an order.

Order Service
Creates the order.
Status = PENDING
Inventory Service
Reserves the items.
Payment Service
Charges the customer.
Shipping Service
Creates a shipment.

If Payment Service fails:

Inventory Service releases the reserved items.
Order Service changes the order status to CANCELLED.

Instead of rolling back a database transaction, each service performs its own compensating action.


Types of Saga Pattern


1. Choreography-based Saga

Services communicate using events. There is no central coordinator.

Example flow:

Order Service
    |
    | OrderCreated
    v
Inventory Service
    |
    | InventoryReserved
    v
Payment Service
    |
    | PaymentProcessed
    v
Shipping Service

Advantages

Loosely coupled
Easy to add new services
No single point of failure

Disadvantages

Difficult to understand as the system grows
Event chains become complex
Harder to debug and monitor



2. Orchestration-based Saga

A central Saga Orchestrator coordinates the workflow.

        Saga Orchestrator
             |
     -------------------
     |       |        |
     v       v        v
 Order   Inventory  Payment
             |
             v
         Shipping

Workflow:

Orchestrator tells Order Service to create an order.
Order Service responds.
Orchestrator tells Inventory Service to reserve stock.
Orchestrator tells Payment Service to charge.
If any step fails, the orchestrator invokes compensating actions.

Advantages

Easier to understand
Centralized workflow
Better monitoring and debugging

Disadvantages

Orchestrator can become complex
Slightly tighter coupling


Compensating Transactions

Every local transaction should have a corresponding undo action.

Transaction	Compensation

Create Order	Cancel Order

Reserve Inventory	Release Inventory

Charge Payment	Refund Payment


Create Shipment	Cancel Shipment




Best Practices
Make all operations idempotent.
Use a message broker (e.g., Kafka or RabbitMQ).
Store saga state persistently.
Implement retry policies with exponential backoff.
Use distributed tracing (e.g., OpenTelemetry).
Clearly define compensating transactions for every step.




2PC → all participants commit or rollback together.
Saga → each service commits its local transaction, and failures are handled using compensating transactions.



Simple example

Suppose you have:

Order Service
      |
      v
Payment Service
      |
      v
Inventory Service

You want all three operations to succeed, or none of them should commit.

2PC has two phases:

Phase 1 — Prepare

A Coordinator asks all participants:

"Can you commit this transaction?"

Coordinator
    |
    | Prepare?
    v
Order     → YES
Payment   → YES
Inventory → YES

If everyone says YES, move to Phase 2.

Phase 2 — Commit

Coordinator says:

Coordinator
    |
    | COMMIT
    v
Order
Payment
Inventory

Everyone commits.

If anyone says NO:

Payment → NO

Coordinator tells everyone:

ROLLBACK
In short
Phase 1: "Are you ready to commit?"
Phase 2: "Okay, everyone commit."
